+JSON: `datom-segments-off.json` / `datom-segments-on.json` (pair 2) in the results directory.
+
+**These A/B numbers were taken on a loaded machine.** Three other experiments were running
+their own benchmarks at the same time (load average 9, two competing `datalog_bench`
+processes). Absolute times drifted upward across the three pairs by up to 2x, which is why the
+off column here is slower than the quiet-machine sweep above. Interleaving keeps the
+comparison fair for the short queries - every pair shows the same 2-3x - but it does not
+rescue `three_hop_count`, the one query long enough for the load to change underneath it.
+
+Five extra fast pairs of `three_hop_count` alone (RUNS=1, alternating) give off/on ratios of
+1.05, 1.07, 0.67, 0.98, 1.40 - noise, centred on no difference. The quiet sweep had it at
+14383 ms off vs 3661 ms on. Those two results cannot both be right, and this machine cannot
+tell them apart today. Treat `three_hop_count` as unmeasured.
 
 Row counts are identical to the baseline for all ten queries at every size.
 
 ## Verdict
 
-TBD
+Worth pursuing, with the caveat that the headline number is not yet trustworthy.
+
+What is solid: every query that spends its time walking an index got 2-3x faster, in every
+run, under every load condition, and a point lookup (`neighbors_of_42`) got more than 10x
+faster. The mechanism is not subtle - 45953 keys become 224, so a scan that was 45953 LSM key
+lookups becomes 224 lookups plus in-memory binary search over a decoded `Vec<Bytes>`. Storage
+is 8% more bytes for 200x fewer keys.
+
+What is not solid: `three_hop_count`, the heaviest query, and therefore the one the numbers
+should have been strongest on. The measurement is contaminated (see above).
+
+Integration cost is moderate, and lower than it looks. The read side was mechanical: one
+cursor type, and every reader kept its logic. The write side is where the care goes - two
+bugs, both silent at size 1 and both about which segment owns a key at a boundary; a real
+version wants property tests over random insert orders, not the fixed pattern the layout test
+uses. The genuinely new obligation is CDC: a segment write republishes datoms it did not
+author, so every WAL consumer needs the tx-eid filter. Anything else reading the WAL later
+(replication, audit) inherits that rule.
+
+Two things this experiment did not measure and a real change would have to. Write
+amplification under random single-datom writes - the bench ingests in sorted batches, the
+friendly case. And whether a per-database segment size can reach the planner's cardinality
+estimates, which the process-wide `estimate_scale` currently fakes.
 
 ## Notes and limits
 
diff --git a/PROGRESS.md b/PROGRESS.md
index d4a3226..6854304 100644
--- a/PROGRESS.md
+++ b/PROGRESS.md
@@ -16,10 +16,12 @@ Datomic-style segments: up to N sorted datom keys under one key (keyed by the la
   - Ten indexer tests and one AE point-`get` asserted the physical one-key-per-datom layout; they now read through `KeyCursor`. No production code does a point `get` on a datom key (checked), only on the version key.
   - The planner's key-count estimate scale is now derived from the env var once per process (`LazyLock`) instead of being written by every `Indexer`, and the three estimate tests scale their expectation. A per-database segment size therefore does not reach the planner - noted as a limitation.
 
